  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1219 AM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nFriday through Tuesday:\n\nNext low pressure system begins to deepen in the lee of the\nColorado Rockies Friday morning, lifting into the northern Plains\nthroughout the day. This will lead to increasing southerly to \nsoutheasterly winds throughout the day, and highs in the low 60s. Low\npressure continues to lift northward into northern \nMinnesota/southwestern Ontario overnight into Saturday morning, \nbringing low temperatures in the upper 40s to near 50 degrees \nFriday night. \n\nGusty southerly winds are expected into Saturday, with increasing\npotential for rain throughout the day. Currently, little to no \nCAPE is indicated in modeling, but even a brief break in cloud \ncover may allow for southwestern Wisconsin to destabilize \nenough to produce isolated lightning. Plenty of low-level shear \nis also available, but any stronger storms would be completely \ndependent on instability developing. Overnight, rainfall \ncontinues as a cold front sweeps through the region, turning \nwinds to northwesterly and remaining gusty as low pressure lifts\ninto James Bay. Overnight lows in the upper 30s to low 40s \ncurrently expected, but could drop farther into the low 30s if \nthe cold front moves through a few hours earlier. \n\nGusty northwesterly winds continue through Sunday, with any wrap-\naround showers exiting by Sunday afternoon. Highs in the 40s are\nexpected. High pressure builds back in Sunday night from the\nnorthern Plains, leading to clearing skies and falling\ntemperatures into the mid-20s. \n\nHigh pressure dominates Monday through Tuesday, with light winds\nand highs in the low 40s and lows in the 20s. Winds gradually\nshift to southeasterly and begin to increase on Tuesday as high\npressure shifts into the Northeast U.S.\n\nMH",
